My comments:

This approach is not what I originally had in mind. It calculates demand by decile with zero bias, calculates bias based on that result (first for the region by comparing to the FAO data, then spreading it over deciles), then recalculates demand by decile with that non-zero bias. it then recalculates the bias (first for the region again by comparing to FAO, then spreading it over deciles), and adds it to the demand to get the final demand level by decile. 

That demand by decile is what is passed to GCAM (without any bias information). GCAM then calculates a bias adder by decile for the base year by calculating demand with zero bias and subtracting from this demand passed from the data system.

To me this is unlikely to make much difference to the way we were doing it before, which was the same but without the additional iteration in calculating demand. The only thing that will be different in this approach is a somewhat different distribution of demand over deciles; in both the new and old approach the demand for the region will be the same (equal to FAO). I doubt that the distribution will be significantly different. Kanishka thinks it might, and therefore will substantially reduce the problem of inconsistencies/jumps in demand when we move from the base year to the first calibrated year.

To me the real issue is still that GCAM will be calculating demand in the base year with zero bias, meaning it will have the wrong elasticities (which depend on the budget shares). Instead, what should happen is that the data system should solve for the bias adders that, when passed to the demand function, make predicted demand equal to the FAO total (and regional bias can then be spread over deciles as we've been doing). Both the demand by decile and the bias adders by decile should be passed to GCAM. In the base year, GCAM can simply adopt the decile-level demand as its calibrated values, or it can calculate demand itself, passing the decile-level bias adders to the demand function. That should produce the same results. All future years in a projection should use the bias adders passed from the data system.

Kanishka wants me to demonstrate what the current problem is, and whether it is reduced much by his new approach. I should do this by selecting a set of parameter values that produces a substantial difference in predicted demand in the base year in my current code, which demonstrates the initial problem, and then run it again with the new GCAM results using his new data system method.

